{#
   THE RFID RESULT — what the server decided and what the reader showed (S141f).

   ⚠️ **Two pages print this and they had drifted.** `/admin/access-rfid-logs`
   and `/admin/utilisateurs/{id}` both list badge scans; both had an "Authorised
   yes/no" column beside a "Status" column, which are the same fact at two
   resolutions, and both printed the status RAW — `REQUIRED_BADGE_MISSING`, an
   upper-snake enum from an older generation of this table. Both also printed it
   twice, because `MachineAccessService` writes the same value into `status` and
   into `reason`. One partial now, so the vocabulary cannot drift again.

   Parameters:

     status      the stored value
     authorized  the boolean the server recorded
     color       the LED the reader lit, if any

   ⚠️ **The fallback humanises, it never prints a key.** Two vocabularies live in
   this table — today's `missing_badge` and the legacy `REQUIRED_BADGE_MISSING` —
   and a row written by a firmware nobody has updated has to read as words, not
   as `rfid_logs.status_WHATEVER`. Twig resolves a missing key to the key itself,
   so `|trans` on a computed key is exactly the wrong tool here.

   ⚠️ The LED dot stays and its literal word does not: what the reader SHOWED the
   member is a different fact from what the server decided, but "green" beside a
   green dot is the dot said twice.
#}
{% set _labels = {
    'authorized': 'rfid_logs.status_authorized',
    'no_badge_required': 'rfid_logs.status_no_badge_required',
    'missing_badge': 'rfid_logs.status_missing_badge',
    'REQUIRED_BADGE_MISSING': 'rfid_logs.status_missing_badge',
    'unknown_rfid': 'rfid_logs.status_unknown_rfid',
    'unknown_machine': 'rfid_logs.status_unknown_machine',
    'invalid_payload': 'rfid_logs.status_invalid_payload',
    'unauthorized_device': 'rfid_logs.status_unauthorized_device',
    'server_error': 'rfid_logs.status_server_error',
    'reader_inactive': 'rfid_logs.status_reader_inactive',
} %}
{% set _key = _labels[status|default('')]|default('') %}
{% include 'site/_cell_state.html.twig' with {
    label: _key ? _key|trans : (status|default('')|lower|replace({'_': ' '})),
    signal: authorized|default(false)
        ? 'go'
        : (status|default('') in ['missing_badge', 'REQUIRED_BADGE_MISSING'] ? 'caution' : 'stop'),
} only %}
{#- ⚠️ **S147, J-6 — the LED colour is DATA, and data does not belong in a
    `style` attribute.** `log.color` is what the physical reader reported, and it
    was interpolated straight into `background:` — 68 of the 78 inline styles on
    `/admin/utilisateurs/{id}` were this one line, and a value the firmware
    invents tomorrow would land in the stylesheet unreviewed. The four the readers
    actually emit (green, orange, red, purple — checked in the table) become
    modifier classes in `components.css`; anything else still draws a dot, in a
    neutral tone, and keeps the raw word in its tooltip so nothing is lost. -#}
{%- if color|default('') %}
    {%- set _known = ['green', 'orange', 'red', 'purple'] -%}
    {%- set _tone = color|lower in _known ? color|lower : 'unknown' -%}
    <span class="color-dot color-dot--{{ _tone }}" title="{{ color }}"></span>
{%- endif %}
